# 🧠 Lab 1 – MDPs, GridWorld & Value Function Approximation

**Course:** EE 5531 – Reinforcement Learning Based Control  
**Institution:** Indian Institute of Technology Palakkad

[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.x-blue)](https://www.python.org/)
[![PyTorch](https://img.shields.io/badge/PyTorch-Deep%20Learning-red)](https://pytorch.org/)
[![Jupyter](https://img.shields.io/badge/Jupyter-Notebook-orange)](https://jupyter.org/)

---

## 🚀 Overview

This lab introduces the fundamental concepts of **Reinforcement Learning** through both theoretical exercises and practical implementation.

The lab focuses on **Markov Decision Processes (MDPs)**, GridWorld environments, stochastic transitions, and basic **value function approximation using PyTorch**.

---

## ⚙️ Key Concepts

- Markov Decision Processes (MDPs)
- States, actions, rewards and transitions
- Deterministic and stochastic environments
- Markov property
- Agent–environment interaction
- PyTorch tensors and operations
- Automatic differentiation
- Finite-difference gradient verification
- Value function approximation
- Neural-network based learning
- Learning-rate comparison

---

## 🧩 Implementations

The programming part of the lab includes:

- Custom **5 × 5 GridWorld** environment
- Boundary and blocked-cell handling
- Goal-state rewards
- Stochastic action transitions
- Empirical transition probability estimation
- MDP formulation of the environment
- PyTorch tensor operations
- Automatic gradient computation
- Numerical gradient verification
- Neural-network value function
- Training using MSE loss and Adam optimizer
- Training-loss visualization
- Learning-rate experiments

---

## 📊 Highlights

- Implemented an RL environment from scratch
- Compared deterministic and stochastic state transitions
- Verified theoretical transition probabilities experimentally
- Compared automatic differentiation with finite differences
- Trained a neural network to approximate a value function
- Studied the effect of different learning rates on convergence

---

## 🎯 Outcome

This lab builds the foundation for understanding how an RL problem can be formulated as an **MDP**, implemented computationally, and combined with **function approximation**.

It provides the groundwork for more advanced topics such as **Monte Carlo methods, Temporal-Difference learning, Deep Reinforcement Learning, and policy/value-based algorithms** covered later in the course.

---

## 📚 Reference

Sutton, R. S. & Barto, A. G.  
*Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction*, 2nd Edition.

